---
import InfoLayout from '../../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
---
<InfoLayout
  title="Pitch a guide"
  description="How to propose a guest guide for Auspicious Music: what the publication accepts, what it declines, and how the editorial review works."
  canonicalPath="/contribute/"
  kicker="Publication"
  lede="Auspicious Music publishes a small number of guest guides — written by people who do the work, edited to the same standard as everything else here."
>
  <h2 style="margin-top:0">What gets published</h2>
  <p>
    A guest guide has the same job as every other page on this site: it helps a
    reader make one real decision in music production, recording, sound design
    or music for media — and it shows its method. That means:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The guide starts from a concrete task, not from a keyword.</li>
    <li>Claims are source-labelled: tested first-hand, taken from documentation, or clearly marked as judgement.</li>
    <li>You are named as the author, with a short bio. Real name, real work.</li>
    <li>The text is edited here before publication — like every other page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is declined, every time</h2>
  <p>
    This publication receives the same outreach every site does, so the line is
    stated plainly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SEO placements.</strong> No paid links, no link exchanges, no "sponsored guest posts" — at any price. This site is not part of any link network and does not intend to become one.</li>
    <li><strong>Link-first articles.</strong> Links inside a guide must earn their place editorially. Commercial links are removed or marked <code style="font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:0.92em">rel="nofollow"</code> / <code style="font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:0.92em">rel="sponsored"</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Mass-produced text.</strong> Generic AI output, spun content and "10 best plugins" listicles without first-hand use.</li>
    <li><strong>Product promotion dressed as advice.</strong> If you have a relationship with a product you mention, it is disclosed on the page or the piece is declined.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Why so strict?</strong>
    <p>
      Unmoderated guest posting is the classic entry point for link spam, and
      readers can tell. Every page here carries the same promise — what was
      tested, researched or preserved. A guest byline does not suspend that.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>How to pitch</h2>
  <p>
    Send an email — a pitch, not a finished article. Three short points are
    enough:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The decision.</strong> What real task does the guide help with? One sentence.</li>
    <li><strong>The method.</strong> What did you test, use or research first-hand? What are the limits of what you can claim?</li>
    <li><strong>Who you are.</strong> A line about your work, and one link to something you have made.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <a class="btn btn--primary" href="mailto:info@matthiasramahi.de?subject=Pitch%20%E2%80%94%20Auspicious%20Music">Pitch by email</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Replies usually take a few days. A pitch may be declined without a detailed
    review — this is a small publication and curation is the point.
  </p>

  <h2>What authors get</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A named byline and a short author bio on the guide.</li>
    <li>Editing that treats the piece as writing, not as inventory.</li>
    <li>A page that stays reachable — the site keeps its archive stable and links honestly.</li>
    <li>No fee in either direction: the publication does not pay for guides and does not accept payment for publishing them.</li>
  </ul>
</InfoLayout>
